PENDING PATCH — launcher preflight + bounded restart (run-hidden.bat)
Written 2026-09-12. NOT APPLIED. NOT TESTED — cmd.exe cannot be run from the agent
sandbox, and the sandbox was down entirely for this session.

WHAT `node --check` ACTUALLY COVERS — CORRECTED 2026-09-13, BEFORE REAPPLYING
  This patch previously implied that a preflight of `node --check` catches "a parse
  error before any gate runs". It catches LESS than that, and the correction comes
  from a live near-miss: on 2026-09-13 a scripted edit left `suppressNotify`
  referenced but never declared inside pollCadence. `node --check` PASSED — it is a
  syntax check, and an undeclared reference inside a function body is a runtime
  ReferenceError, not a syntax error. Only executing the function found it.
  Three layers, and the preflight provides only the first:
    1. node --check <file>          SYNTAX only. Catches the dropped brace. Misses
                                    every reference error.
    2. dry import                   `node -e "import('./src/index.js')"` with the
                                    poll loop gated off. Catches MODULE-SCOPE errors
                                    — bad imports, undeclared top-level references,
                                    circular-import failures.
    3. execution                    Function-body ReferenceErrors. Only the boot
                                    self-test (admitSelfTest) or test-delivery.js
                                    reaches these.
  A launcher preflight can afford 1 and arguably 2 (a dry import costs one process
  and no network if the poll loop is gated). It CANNOT afford 3. So the patch must
  claim layer 1 (+2 if implemented) and say plainly that a ReferenceError inside a
  poller still reaches production and is caught by the suite, not by the launcher.
  Shipping it with wider stated coverage than it has would be the fifth instance of
  the class this project keeps fixing: a guard that reports more assurance than it
  provides.

THE HOLE THIS CLOSES
  Every gate in this project assumes the process reaches main(). admitSelfTest, the
  tier-route assertion, the classifier-wiring check and the pagination guard all run
  INSIDE index.js. A parse error in any statically-imported module throws at import
  time, before the first line of main(), so none of them execute. The only gate that
  can run earlier than the code it checks is the launcher.

CURRENT LAUNCHER (verified by reading it, 2026-09-12):
  @echo off
  cd /d "%~dp0"
  :loop
  node src\index.js >> data\bot.log 2>&1
  echo [%date% %time%] bot exited, restarting in 15s... >> data\bot.log
  timeout /t 15 /nobreak >nul
  goto loop
  -> UNBOUNDED. A parse error = 4 crashes/minute, forever, with no operator line.

WHY THIS IS PARKED AND NOT APPLIED
  1. It cannot be tested here. The file header of PUSH-TO-GITHUB.bat already records
     the rule, learned twice: "cmd.exe cannot be tested from the agent sandbox, so
     stop writing clever batch." Two parser casualties are documented there (nested
     PowerShell quoting; delims=' colliding with an apostrophe), and a third was hit
     on 2026-09-07 — a literal ")" inside a parenthesised if-block closed it early
     and cmd abandoned the rest of the script SILENTLY.
  2. The suggested form contains exactly that hazard:
       for /r src %%f in (*.js) do (
         node --check "%%f" || ( echo ... & exit /b 1 )
       )
     The inner ")" terminates the outer "do (" block. Nested parens plus "&" inside
     an if/|| are the two constructs that have broken this project's batch before.
  3. THE FAILURE MODE IS WORSE THAN THE ONE IT PREVENTS. A buggy preflight that
     always fails means the bot NEVER STARTS — silent, permanent, and possibly
     without even writing its log line. That is strictly worse than a crash-loop,
     which at least leaves a visible trail in bot.log. Shipping an untested launcher
     tonight, with MOVE's and ORDER's windows closing, would repeat the exact
     mistake the demote-scope patch was just reverted to avoid — in the one file
     that is MORE load-bearing than the app.

PREFLIGHT DESIGN, WRITTEN BATCH-SAFE (apply and test with a deliberate syntax error)
  No nested parentheses. No "&" command chaining. errorlevel captured immediately,
  because echo can reset it.

--- begin run-hidden.bat ---
@echo off
cd /d "%~dp0"
set "BACKOFF=15"

:preflight
set "BAD="
for /r "%~dp0src" %%f in (*.js) do call :check "%%f"
rem Root-level readers the bot imports dynamically (cadence-watch imports
rem detect-cliff-cluster.js at runtime). Cheap to include; a parse error there is
rem non-fatal today but still wrong.
call :check "detect-cadence.js"
call :check "detect-cliff-cluster.js"
call :check "discover-vesting.js"
if defined BAD goto refuse
goto run

:check
node --check %1 2>nul
set "RC=%errorlevel%"
if "%RC%"=="0" exit /b
echo [%date% %time%] [PREFLIGHT][OPERATOR] parse error in %1 >> data\bot.log
set "BAD=1"
exit /b

:refuse
echo [%date% %time%] [PREFLIGHT][OPERATOR] REFUSING TO START - a source file does not parse. Fix it, then start the bot. This does NOT retry, because a parse error does not heal itself and a 15s loop would hide it. >> data\bot.log
exit /b 1

:run
node src\index.js >> data\bot.log 2>&1
echo [%date% %time%] bot exited, restarting in %BACKOFF%s... >> data\bot.log
timeout /t %BACKOFF% /nobreak >nul
rem BOUNDED BY BACKOFF, NOT BY A CAP. A hard stop after N tries leaves an unattended
rem machine with no bot at all, which is the wrong trade for a transient fault. This
rem escalates 15 -> 60 -> 300s and stays at 300s, so a persistent failure costs 12
rem restarts an hour instead of 240 and is obvious in the log, while a one-off crash
rem still recovers in 15 seconds.
if "%BACKOFF%"=="15" set "BACKOFF=60" & goto preflight
if "%BACKOFF%"=="60" echo [%date% %time%] [OPERATOR] third restart - backing off to 5 minutes. Something is failing repeatedly. >> data\bot.log
if "%BACKOFF%"=="60" set "BACKOFF=300"
goto preflight
--- end run-hidden.bat ---

  NOTE the "set X=Y & goto" line above still uses "&" — REWRITE IT as two lines
  before applying. It is left visible here as the thing to fix, not as a template.

GAP 1 — A CLEAN REFUSAL IS THE MOST SILENT FAILURE OF ALL
  The heartbeat reports "the bot is down" only while the bot is up. So the SAFEST
  failure mode is also the one nothing announces: one line in a log file on a machine
  nobody is reading. Refusing to start without a notification trades an invisible
  crash-loop for an invisible refusal.
  The notification must therefore live OUTSIDE the Node process, in the launcher,
  working precisely when the process cannot run.

  SCOPE NOTE, because it touches Telegram: this is the BOT's own delivery path,
  extended to its launcher. It is explicitly permitted — CLAUDE.md restricts CLAUDE
  from reading any Telegram surface and says in terms that "the bot's own delivery is
  unaffected". Nothing here reads a chat, a channel or a message; it only sends. The
  token is read from .env by PowerShell and never printed to the log.

  IT MUST GO TO THE OPERATOR DM, NOT THE CHANNEL. A preflight refusal is telemetry,
  and v0.31.0 split telemetry out of @radaralert22. The chat id is the subscriber in
  data/state.json, which PowerShell can parse directly.

  DO NOT INLINE THE POWERSHELL. Nested PowerShell quoting inside a .bat is casualty
  #1 in PUSH-TO-GITHUB.bat's header. This project already keeps .ps1 files
  (wake-timers.ps1, wake-selftest.ps1) — follow that precedent:

--- begin notify-operator.ps1 ---
param([Parameter(Mandatory=$true)][string]$Text)
# Launcher-side notifier. Runs when the bot CANNOT — a parse error, a refusal to
# start — so it must never throw, never block, and never write the token anywhere.
try {
  $envFile = Join-Path $PSScriptRoot '.env'
  $token = (Select-String -Path $envFile -Pattern '^TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=(.+)$').Matches[0].Groups[1].Value.Trim()
  $state = Get-Content (Join-Path $PSScriptRoot 'data\state.json') -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json
  $chat  = $state.subscribers[0]
  if (-not $token -or -not $chat) { exit 0 }
  $body = @{ chat_id = $chat; text = $Text } | ConvertTo-Json -Compress
  Invoke-RestMethod -Method Post -TimeoutSec 20 -ContentType 'application/json' `
    -Uri "https://api.telegram.org/bot$token/sendMessage" -Body $body | Out-Null
} catch {
  # A failed notification must not stop the launcher doing its job. The log line in
  # bot.log is the fallback record; this is best-effort on top of it.
  exit 0
}
exit 0
--- end notify-operator.ps1 ---

  Then in :refuse, AFTER the log line:
    powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "%~dp0notify-operator.ps1" "[PREFLIGHT] Market Radar refused to start: a source file does not parse. The bot is DOWN and will not retry. See data\bot.log."

  TEST IT BY BREAKING IT: confirm the DM arrives with the bot stopped, and confirm a
  deliberately wrong token still lets the launcher exit cleanly rather than hang.

GAP 2 — BACKOFF MUST RESET AFTER A SUSTAINED RUN
  As drafted it escalates 15 -> 60 -> 300s and never comes back down, so one bad
  night leaves the launcher at five minutes indefinitely and a routine restart takes
  five minutes to recover. Reset on evidence that the process actually ran.

  Batch cannot do elapsed time without pain (midnight rollover, locale). PowerShell
  is already a dependency here, and this form avoids the "%" escaping trap entirely
  (no -UFormat %s, which needs doubling inside a .bat):

    for /f %%t in ('powershell -NoProfile -Command "[int](New-TimeSpan -Start (Get-Date '1970-01-01') -End (Get-Date).ToUniversalTime()).TotalSeconds"') do set "T0=%%t"
    node src\index.js >> data\bot.log 2>&1
    for /f %%t in ('powershell -NoProfile -Command "[int](New-TimeSpan -Start (Get-Date '1970-01-01') -End (Get-Date).ToUniversalTime()).TotalSeconds"') do set "T1=%%t"
    set /a RAN=T1-T0
    if %RAN% GEQ 600 set "BACKOFF=15"

  A run of 10 minutes or more counts as "it worked, then something happened" and
  restores fast recovery; anything shorter is a crash-loop and keeps escalating.
  600s is a DECLARED constant, not a derived one — record it as such.

TEST PLAN (all of it, before trusting this)
  1. Back up run-hidden.bat first.
  2. Apply. Start it. Confirm the bot boots normally and bot.log shows the usual
     banner — i.e. the preflight passes on a healthy tree and costs only the extra
     seconds of ~100 `node --check` spawns.
  3. Introduce a deliberate parse error in a leaf module (e.g. an unclosed brace in
     src/core/pulse.js). Start it. Confirm EXACTLY one [PREFLIGHT][OPERATOR] line
     naming that file, and NO restart loop.
  4. Revert the deliberate error. Confirm normal boot again.
  5. Kill the node process mid-run. Confirm the backoff escalates and logs.
  Only after 3 and 5 actually behave is this better than what it replaces.

COST TO WEIGH
  ~100 `node --check` spawns on Windows adds perhaps 10-30s to every start,
  including every restart in the backoff loop. Narrowing to src/ plus the three root
  readers keeps it near 40 files. Measure before accepting.
